<edge source="global_rate_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="ai_safety_race::frontier_dev_speed" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher expected policy rates raise the effective cost of equity and debt for AI firms and investors, compressing venture and corporate funding and causing firms to postpone hires, large training runs and multi‑quarter R&amp;D projects, which slows frontier development.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_rate_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_safety_race</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="brics_alignment_growth::cross_border_capital" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher expected short-term policy rates in major central banks reprices institutional portfolios and draws yield-sensitive flows toward higher‑rate jurisdictions, increasing the volume and directional intensity of cross-border capital movements.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_rate_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">brics_alignment_growth</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="gulf_energy_flashpoint::global_oil_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising market-implied policy rates attract capital into the dollar and raise discount rates, reducing demand for commodities and putting downward pressure on dollar‑priced crude benchmarks within weeks.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_rate_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">gulf_energy_flashpoint</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_rate_shock::policy_rate_expectations" target="russia_ukraine_conflict::diplomatic_pressure_leve" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Higher expected policy rates raise projected borrowing costs and fiscal strain for Western governments, shifting political priorities toward domestic economic stabilisation and reducing appetite and bandwidth for sustained high‑intensity diplomatic pressure campaigns over the medium term.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">global_rate_shock</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
</edge>
